WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.909
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.839
They say 1% can solve this problem.

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:11.369
You need to select three of the balls and place them in the circles,

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:15.059
such that the numbers on the balls have a sum of 30.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:21.629
You're allowed to use a number more than once but you must use exactly three of the balls.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:29.189
This problem was sent to me by email and there are many popular variations of this puzzle across the internet.

00:00:29.189 --> 00:00:32.459
I thought this was a fun variation I wanted to share.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:39.570
Pause the video if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.520
Recall the rules of parity. An even number plus or minus an even number will always be an even number. An

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.660
even number plus or minus an odd number will always be an odd number.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.890
Finally, an odd number plus or minus an odd number will always be an even number. In this problem,

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:16.099
we're only dealing with sums. In particular, we want the sum of three odd numbers. To get that,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.820
we'll add an odd number to both sides of the very last equation.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:27.320
We end up with the result of an even number plus an odd number which we know from the second rule is equal to an

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.970
odd number. Therefore, the sum of three odd numbers will always be an odd number.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:40.519
So, how can we get three numbers to have a sum of 30 if we're limited to the odd numbers from 1 to 15? We

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.520
can't! It's mathematically impossible.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:48.169
But this is not strictly a mathematical problem. This is a puzzle.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:56.479
So what's the trick? The clue is that the balls have the same colors as billiard balls. Take a look at the green colored ball.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.850
We can orient it in

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:00.720
three-dimensional space. In

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:07.609
particular, you might know the trivia that the green colored ball in a standard set of billiard balls is the number 6.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.359
We turn the 9 upside down to get the even number 6.

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:16.789
We then solve the puzzle by adding the numbers 11 and 13.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:22.639
6 plus 11 plus 13 is equal to 30, and that's the answer to this puzzle.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:29.569
There's an important lesson: When a problem seems impossible, try changing your perspective!

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:37.999
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube. As always, thanks for watching, and thanks for your support.

